# Keep the Docker build context small + the layer cache stable: exclude local
# build artifacts, VCS metadata, and editor/OS cruft. The builder runs a clean
# `cargo build --locked` over the source, so none of these are needed in-context.

# Rust build output (large; rebuilt in the builder stage).
# `/target` is anchored and so misses nested ones: `crates/core/fuzz` is its own workspace
# with its own `target/`. `**/target` catches those; no directory named `target` is ever an
# input to this image.
**/target
**/*.rs.bk

# Local cargo config. `.cargo/config.toml` is gitignored and its own header says it is not
# part of the repo's build contract, but `COPY . .` would make it exactly that: a local
# `rustflags` linker override would enter the builder, which carries no such toolchain, and
# fail the build with a misleading linker error. Machine-shaped build flags must not enter
# the image. Scoped to the file, not the directory: `.cargo/mutants.toml` is tracked.
.cargo/config.toml

# Tool scratch space inside the checkout. It can hold whole working copies of this repo,
# each with its own `target/`. Besides the transfer cost, a build context should never
# contain another checkout's source.
.claude/

# VCS + CI metadata.
.git
.github
.gitignore

# Generated CI artifacts (never part of a build).
*.cdx.json
/lcov.info

# Local key material / scratch (never baked into an image).
*.c4gh
*.c4gh.pub
# Local env files may carry secrets — never let them flow into the builder's `COPY . .`.
# Both a bare name and a glob: Docker matches these patterns with Go's `filepath.Match`
# against the whole relative path, with no prefix semantics, so `.env` alone would exclude
# `.env` (a single COMPOSE_PROFILES line, no credential) and still let `.env.external`
# through — the file `.env.external.example` says "may carry credentials", and which
# `.gitignore` excludes for that reason.
.env
.env.*
# The operator's filled service/provider configs, excluded for the same reason as the env
# files above and by `.gitignore`'s own words: they "hold a Vault token and S3 access keys
# by design". They are the default discovery names, and README/docs/deployment.md tell
# operators to create them, so without these lines `COPY . .` carries a live Vault token
# and S3 credentials into the `builder` layer. The final runtime image is unaffected (it
# only `COPY --from=binsrc` the binary and the licences), so the exposure is the builder
# layer: `--target builder`, a BuildKit `--cache-to` registry export, or `docker history`
# on it. Neither file is ever a build input, so excluding them cannot break the image.
#
# Anchored (`/node.toml`, not `node.toml`), matching .gitignore: only the checkout-root
# file is the credential-bearing one. Docker strips the leading `/` and matches the
# remainder against the context-relative path, so this excludes exactly the root file —
# the shipped `<SET ME: …>` templates (`node.example.toml`, which the Dockerfile needs)
# and any same-named file deeper in the tree keep flowing into the build.
/node.toml
/tool.toml

# Deployment/packaging artifacts that are not build inputs. Keep this list complete: an
# omitted compose file still lands in the context, so editing it busts the `COPY . .` layer
# and forces a full cold `cargo build --release` — the exact cost the others avoid.
Dockerfile
Dockerfile.ops
.dockerignore
docker-compose.yml
docker-compose.minimal.yml
docker-compose.observability.yml
docker-compose.s3.yml
docker-compose.chaos.yml
docker-compose.external.yml
compose/
deploy/
scripts/e2e/

# Editor / OS cruft.
.DS_Store
*.swp
